---
// The methodology block. It appears in full on every page that quotes a number,
// because a comparison without its method is marketing, not evidence.
export interface Props { data: any; }
const { data } = Astro.props;
---
<h2 class="section-title" style="margin-top:44px">How these numbers were produced</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Scanner: <b>{data.scanner}</b>, vulnerability DB schema {data.dbSchema}, built {data.dbBuilt}.</li>
  <li>DB checksum: <code class="kv-mono" style="word-break:break-all">{data.dbChecksum}</code></li>
  <li>Platform: <code class="kv-mono">{data.platform}</code>. Scan date: <b>{data.scanDate}</b>.</li>
  <li>Production <code class="kv-mono">latest</code> tags were resolved to immutable digests before scanning, so the run is repeatable.</li>
  <li>Every image was scanned with the same Grype binary and the same frozen database.</li>
  <li>Raw Grype findings. No vendor VEX suppression was applied to anyone, including us.</li>
  <li>Only publicly pullable images were scanned. No paid or private registry credentials were used.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  The full {data.csvRows}-row dataset, including the resolved reference and digest for every
  provider/image pair, is here: <a href={data.csvPath}>cve-comparison-{data.scanDate}.csv</a>.
  Every figure on this page is derived from that file.
</p>
<h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;margin-top:28px">What this does not prove</h3>
<p>
  <code class="kv-mono">latest</code> does not guarantee the same application version or feature
  set across vendors, so an individual outlier should be version- and feature-matched before it
  is treated as a product-level claim. A scanner reports known findings visible to one database
  snapshot; a low count is not proof that an image is free of vulnerabilities. An image a vendor
  does not publish is counted as <b>not available</b> — never as zero.
</p>
